# Sector Tagger Project Rules

## Project Overview

- NPM package name: sector-tagger-svelte
- GitHub account: PaznerA
- Tech stack: Bun + Astro + Svelte 5 + SQLite + WebSocket
- Purpose: Visual editor for creating, managing and tagging sectored layouts

## Core Features

### Canvas Management
- Single canvas instance for performance
- Hierarchical rendering (pages -> views -> sectors)
- Proper z-index handling for overlapping elements
- Real-time synchronization via WebSocket
- Offline support with LocalStorage

### Sector Types
1. Page
   - Top-level container
   - Blue color scheme
   - Contains views
2. View
   - Mid-level layout section
   - Green color scheme
   - Contains sectors
3. Sector
   - Lowest-level content area
   - Red color scheme
   - No children allowed

### Interactions
- Click to select
- Drag to move
- Resize from corners
- Hover effects
- Transform mode for advanced editing
- Real-time updates across browsers

### Hierarchy Rules
- Pages can contain views
- Views can contain sectors
- Sectors are leaf nodes
- Children move with parents
- Transformations cascade down the tree

### UI Components
- Canvas (main editing area)
- SelectedPanel (details of selected item)
- HoverPanel (quick info on hover)
- Controls (transform toggle)
- TransformHandle (resize/rotate handles)

### Visual Feedback
- Color coding by type
- Selection highlight
- Hover state
- Resize handles
- Parent-child relationships
- Real-time sync status

## Development Rules
1. Use trailing commas in multiline
2. Keep code in English
3. Document in English
4. Maintain type safety
5. Follow Svelte 5 runes best practices:
   - Use `let` for reactive variables
   - Use `$effect` for side effects
   - Use `$derived` for computed values
   - Use `$props` for component props
   - Use `$state` for complex state
6. Keep performance in mind
7. Use environment variables for configuration

